To rationalize the denominator, I want to get rid of the cube roots by multiplying the fraction by a suitable expression.

I notice that the denominator is the sum of two cube roots, so I can use the formula $(a+b)(a^2-ab+b^2)=a^3+b^3$ to get rid of the cubes.

I can choose $a=\sqrt[3]{4}$ and $b=\sqrt[3]{32}$, since $a^3=4$ and $b^3=32$.

So, I multiply the fraction by $\frac{a^2-ab+b^2}{a^2-ab+b^2}$, which is equivalent to 1, and I get

\begin{align*}
\frac{2}{\sqrt[3]{4}+\sqrt[3]{32}} \cdot \frac{a^2-ab+b^2}{a^2-ab+b^2} &= \frac{2(a^2-ab+b^2)}{a^3+b^3} \\
&= \frac{2(\sqrt[3]{4}^2-\sqrt[3]{4}\sqrt[3]{32}+\sqrt[3]{32}^2)}{4+32} \\
&= \frac{2(\sqrt[3]{16}-\sqrt[3]{128}+\sqrt[3]{1024})}{36} \\
&= \frac{\sqrt[3]{16}-\sqrt[3]{128}+\sqrt[3]{1024}}{18}
\end{align*}

Now the denominator is rational, but the numerator still has cube roots.

I want to simplify the numerator as much as possible, so I look for factors of 16, 128, and 1024 that are perfect cubes.

I notice that 8 is a perfect cube, and it divides 16, 128, and 1024.

So I can factor out 8 from each term and get

\begin{align*}
\frac{\sqrt[3]{16}-\sqrt[3]{128}+\sqrt[3]{1024}}{18} &= \frac{\sqrt[3]{8}\sqrt[3]{2}-\sqrt[3]{8}\sqrt[3]{16}+\sqrt[3]{8}\sqrt[3]{128}}{18} \\
&= \frac{\sqrt[3]{8}(\sqrt[3]{2}-\sqrt[3]{16}+\sqrt[3]{128})}{18}
\end{align*}

Current Step: This is the simplest form I can get for the fraction, and it matches the form of $\frac{\sqrt[3]{A}}{B}$. Error: <->

