Applying this identity to the equation $\cos (\frac{\pi}{2} - 3 \theta) = \cos 7 \theta$, I get two possible cases:

\begin{align*}
\frac{\pi}{2} - 3 \theta &= 7 \theta + 2 k \pi \tag{1}\\
\frac{\pi}{2} - 3 \theta &= -7 \theta + 2 k \pi \tag{2}
\end{align*}

I can solve for $\theta$ in each case by rearranging the terms and dividing by the coefficients.

