This looks like a system of two equations with two unknowns, x and y. I wonder if I can solve it by elimination or substitution.

If I subtract the first equation from the second, I get

\begin{align*}
2^{x+2}+3^{y+1} - (2^x+3^y) &= 18 - 5\\
2^x(2^2-1)+3^y(3-1) &= 13\\
2^x(3)+3^y(2) &= 13.
\end{align*}

This seems simpler, but I still don't see an obvious way to find x and y. Maybe I can use some properties of exponents to rewrite the equations in a different way.

I notice that both equations have a 2^x term and a 3^y term.

Maybe I can use a substitution like

\begin{align*}
u &= 2^x\\
v &= 3^y
\end{align*}

to turn the equations into something more familiar.

Then the equations become

\begin{align*}
u+v &= 5\\
4u+3v &= 18.
\end{align*}

This looks like a standard linear system that I can solve by elimination.

If I multiply the first equation by 3 and subtract it from the second, I get

\begin{align*}
4u+3v - (3u+3v) &= 18 - (3\times 5)\\
u &= 3.
\end{align*}

This means that

\begin{align*}
2^x = u = 3.
\end{align*}

To find x, I can take the logarithm of both sides, using any base I like.

I'll use base 2 for convenience.

