I recall that the remainder theorem states that the remainder of dividing $p(x)$ by $x - a$ is equal to $p(a).$

So, in this problem, I have two equations:

\begin{align*}
p(-1) &= 5 \\
p(-5) &= -7
\end{align*}

I also know that the division algorithm tells me that I can write $p(x)$ as the product of the divisor and the quotient, plus the remainder.

So, if I let $q(x)$ be the quotient and $r(x)$ be the remainder of dividing $p(x)$ by $(x + 1)(x + 5),$ I have:

\begin{align*}
p(x) &= (x + 1)(x + 5)q(x) + r(x)
\end{align*}

Since the degree of the divisor is 2, the degree of the remainder must be less than 2.

So, the most general form of the remainder is a linear polynomial: $r(x) = ax + b.$

Now, I can use the two equations I have for $p(-1)$ and $p(-5)$ to find the values of $a$ and $b.$

Plugging in $x = -1,$ I get:

\begin{align*}
p(-1) &= (-1 + 1)(-1 + 5)q(-1) + a(-1) + b \\
5 &= 0 + (-a + b) \\
a - b &= -5
\end{align*}

Similarly, plugging in $x = -5,$ I get:

\begin{align*}
p(-5) &= (-5 + 1)(-5 + 5)q(-5) + a(-5) + b \\
-7 &= 0 + (-5a + b) \\
5a - b &= 7
\end{align*}

Now, I have a system of two equations and two unknowns, which I can solve by elimination or substitution.

I will use elimination.

Current Step: Adding the two equations, I get:

\begin{align*}
6a &= 2 \\
a &= \frac{1}{3}
\end{align*} Error: <->

